Detect the UTF-8 BOM with peek() instead of read() and putback().

strip_utf8_bom() read the first three bytes of the .aff and .dic files and,
when they were not a UTF-8 byte order mark, put them back with putback().
With QNX's libc++ an istream::read() of a few bytes from a std::filebuf can
go straight into the caller's buffer, leaving the filebuf no putback
position: putback() then fails and sets badbit, and every dictionary
without a BOM failed to load ("There is no number" in the .dic, "Parsing
error"). The standard does not guarantee putback after read(), so peek at
the first byte instead and only read the three bytes when it is the BOM's
first byte (0xEF). Files with a BOM behave as before.

Upstream-Status: Pending (portable: putback after read() is not guaranteed by the standard)

--- a/src/nuspell/aff_data.cxx
+++ b/src/nuspell/aff_data.cxx
@@ -297,6 +297,15 @@
 {
 	if (!in.good())
 		return;
+	// Look before reading: only a stream that starts with the BOM's first
+	// byte has anything to strip. Putting back characters just read is
+	// not guaranteed to work (std::filebuf may have read them straight
+	// into our buffer, leaving no putback position), and a failed
+	// putback leaves the stream failed.
+	if (in.peek() != std::istream::traits_type::to_int_type('\xEF')) {
+		reset_failbit_istream(in);
+		return;
+	}
 	auto bom = string(3, '\0');
 	in.read(bom.data(), 3);
 	if (in && bom == "\xEF\xBB\xBF")
